<title-page
title: Party Clown
author: Ty

# Any Event

The host of the event gathers the people interested in the clown portion of the event

@ Host

> I Write My Own Lines, Clown!

The host ends by introducing the clown with a sense of flair that matches their personality and the vibe of the event.

@ Clown

> Hi. I'm friends with <casual name of host that lets the guests know i'm informal>. I won't take up much time with logistics.

The clown reveals the MATERIALS

> Count with me. One Hat. Three Pins. Five Rings. 7 Balls. 9 Scarves. 

> To send me on my way, I will need all of these MATERIALS returned to me.

> If we fail to recover the MATERIALS then I will live here now.

> I will need three square meals a day and eight hours of sleep.

> If I get woken up, then my eight hours of slumber will start over.

> And if I sleep too long, there will be no circus that evening.

> And I would only be able to juggle with the MATERIALS we have left, anyways.

> And if after I live out my long full comfortable life in this very nice house

> by the way, thank you so much for having me today and for forever, hopefully not, maybe.

> Eating three square meals a day and getting eight hours of sleep and circusing before bed.

> I digress. If we do not count all of these again and get them back to me, I will be stuck here.

> Count with me. One Hat. Three Pins. Five Rings. 7 Balls. 9 Scarves. 

The clown does walkabout close up practical effects, including but not limited to:

Jugging: hats, pings, rings, balls, scarves
Cards: drawing, throwing, pitching
Consulting: business, computers, comedy
Gaming: Ninjami (digital, physical)

The party is exhausted of clown activities

@ Host

> Time to send the clown on his way, count his stuff please, quick, quick, quick!

The host pays the clown in money and in returned rented MATERIALS.

INVENTORY

- one hat
- three pins
- five rings
- seven balls
- nine scarves

The clown leaves and goes to Prime Clown Circus.

SHOW RUNNER NOTES: Add Prime Clown Circus to Clown Canon as

The premiere event in theatrical storytelling. With common essential materials, a circus can be made and shared anywhered.

This stage showcase can be scaled from a casual children's birthday party to international olympiads of street performers."
